The panel detector built into the app
(assets/models/comicredr-panels.onnx)
-------------------------------------

The model is a fine-tune of D-FINE-S, made with spike/train.py and
released under the Apache License 2.0, like the rest of ComicRedr.

- D-FINE, by Yansong Peng, Hebei Li, Peixi Wu, Yueyi Zhang, Xiaoyan Sun
  and Feng Wu: https://github.com/Peterande/D-FINE (Apache-2.0). The
  starting weights are its COCO-only checkpoint, loaded through the
  Hugging Face transformers port ustc-community/dfine-small-coco
  (Apache-2.0). Its HGNetv2 backbone was pretrained on ImageNet.
- COCO dataset (pretraining): annotations CC BY 4.0, the COCO Consortium,
  https://cocodataset.org.

Fine-tuned on pages from these comics, labelled by the ComicRedr project
(spike/labels/train/, listed with their sources in
test/train.manifest.toml):

- Pepper&Carrot by David Revoy, CC BY 4.0, https://www.peppercarrot.com
- Rogan of the North #7, #8 and #9 by David D. Lanciani, CC BY 4.0
- Bushwalk by Wdking, CC BY 4.0
- The Scream of Joy, The Wood of Holoferm, Mom's Boyfriend, In The Era and
  Taken For A Ride by Norman Bargo, CC BY 4.0
- Men Don't Bark by Norman Bargo, Public Domain Mark 1.0
- Dark Sun Chronicles by Paul Winchester, Public Domain Mark 1.0
- La Traversée d'une semaine by Yvang, CC BY 3.0
- Pickman's Model by Kim Holm (after H. P. Lovecraft), CC BY 3.0
- They Lived Too Long by TREHERN, Public Domain Mark 1.0
- The Dying Shadows by Ruby May Valentine, CC0 1.0
- Public-domain golden- and silver-age comic books (copyright not renewed)
  from Quality, Fiction House, Lev Gleason, MLJ, Novelty, ACG, Charlton,
  Ziff-Davis, Star, Trojan, Comic Media, Avon and others, from scans on
  the Internet Archive
- US government works, in the public domain: the US Army's PS Magazine
  #100 and #108

The screenshots in docs/screenshots/ show Pepper&Carrot by David Revoy
(CC BY 4.0) and public-domain comics.
